$DIR/main.p:13:10: s.f holds a view of v (the function literal, line 11), which was moved into eat (its parameter takes a L by value) at line 12, so s.f can no longer be used here
    s.f, part of s, looks at memory that v owns, without owning it: the view was
    made by the function literal at line 11. A view of an owner held in a local
    variable may be used only while the variable keeps the value the view was
    made from. At line 12 v was moved into eat (its parameter takes a L by
    value), so the view may look at memory that was released, or at a value that
    now belongs to something else.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use s.f before line 12, or move v only after the last use of s.f
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)
